1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:04,880
Your chance of deciding a presidential election: 1 in 60 million.

2
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:08,400
You're more likely to die in a car crash driving to the polls.

3
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:16,120
The expected value of your vote is zero point zero zero zero one seven dollars.

4
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:22,220
The cost of casting it (time, gas, parking) is about fifty dollars.

5
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:29,160
Democracy was designed so that your individual participation is mathematically meaningless.

6
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:36,220
What isn't meaningless: the four point one billion dollars corporations spend annually on lobbying.

7
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:42,180
That math works out just fine. The Founding Fathers were brilliant men.

8
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:46,960
They anticipated tyranny, corruption, foreign invasion, and mob rule.

9
00:00:47,620 --> 00:00:54,000
They did not anticipate Florida. What Democracy Should Look Like vs Reality.

10
00:00:54,840 --> 00:01:01,200
Princeton University analyzed one thousand seven hundred seventy-nine policy decisions over twenty years.

11
00:01:02,020 --> 00:01:06,820
The correlation between what ordinary voters want and what actually happens?

12
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:14,360
Zero. Percent. When economic elites want something? Seventy-eight percent of the time.

13
00:01:14,780 --> 00:01:20,360
You have the same political influence as your houseplant. Slightly less, actually.

14
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:24,500
Your houseplant produces oxygen, which at least one congressman needs.

15
00:01:25,740 --> 00:01:32,680
Your institutions tell the whole story in their names. You have a "Department of Defense" that mainly attacks people.

16
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:36,880
A "Department of Health" that has not yet produced any observable health.

17
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:41,460
And a "Department of Justice" that (you can see where this is going).

18
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:50,300
Whether zero percent or one hundred percent of voters support a bill, it has the same roughly thirty percent chance of passing.

19
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:58,120
Democracy is a slot machine where every pull gives the same result but you keep playing because the machine has an American

20
00:01:58,140 --> 00:02:05,340
flag on it. The Mathematics of Political Failure. Concentrated Benefits vs.

21
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:12,940
Diffuse Costs. Sugar subsidies cost Americans three point five billion dollars per year.

22
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:18,360
That's ten dollars per person. Will you spend weeks fighting to save ten dollars?

23
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:24,740
No. Will sugar companies spend millions to keep their three point five billion dollars?

24
00:02:25,420 --> 00:02:34,820
Yes. Guess who wins? This pattern repeats everywhere: Pharma companies earn five hundred billion dollars in concentrated

25
00:02:34,820 --> 00:02:41,260
profits. Patients pay one thousand five hundred dollars each in diffuse suffering.

26
00:02:42,020 --> 00:02:50,160
The winner is not you. Banks in two thousand eight received a seven hundred billion dollar bailout (concentrated).

27
00:02:50,820 --> 00:02:54,610
You paid two thousand one hundred dollars in taxes (diffuse).

28
00:02:55,520 --> 00:03:05,880
The winner is the ones with yachts. How Money Buys Power. Campaign contributions don't directly buy how politicians vote.

29
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:12,200
Only one in four studies support that. The system is much more elegant than bribery.

30
00:03:12,460 --> 00:03:17,520
Bribery is what poor countries do. Rich countries have "campaign finance.".

31
00:03:18,760 --> 00:03:25,590
But they do something far more effective: they buy which politicians exist in the first place.

32
00:03:26,240 --> 00:03:31,750
Ninety-five percent of House races since two thousand four were won by the highest spender.

33
00:03:32,340 --> 00:03:37,640
The average winning House campaign costs two point seven nine million dollars.

34
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:44,080
The average winning Senate campaign costs twenty-six point five three million dollars.

35
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:50,140
Why bribe a politician when you can just manufacture one who already agrees with you?

36
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:53,500
It's like complaining about the menu when you own the restaurant.

37
00:03:54,640 --> 00:04:02,840
For the ones already in office, contributions buy something more subtle than votes: Access: Your call gets returned, theirs

38
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:08,200
goes to voicemail. Agenda control: Your issue gets a hearing, theirs gets buried.

39
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:12,940
Legislative language: The exact wording that creates loopholes.

40
00:04:13,940 --> 00:04:20,940
Committee positions: four hundred fifty thousand dollars to party committees buys a seat where bills live or die.

41
00:04:21,779 --> 00:04:27,180
You don't buy the vote. You buy the voter, the agenda, and the room where the vote happens.

42
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:34,010
The Lobbying return on investment, or R.O.I.: eighteen to one Returns.

43
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:40,240
The most profitable investment in America isn't stocks, real estate, or crypto.

44
00:04:40,560 --> 00:04:47,220
It's congressmen: Disease advocacy groups spend one hundred million dollars on lobbying.

45
00:04:47,620 --> 00:04:51,540
They get one point eight billion dollars in increased funding.

46
00:04:52,180 --> 00:04:58,680
The R.O.I. is eighteen to one (eighteen hundred percent return).

47
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:03,240
Meanwhile: Breast cancer: Great lobbyists result in massive funding.

48
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:09,120
Heart disease: Kills more people, but worse lobbyists result in less funding.

49
00:05:09,940 --> 00:05:18,280
Rare pediatric diseases: No lobbyists means no funding. Democracy doesn't allocate resources by who's dying.

50
00:05:19,060 --> 00:05:24,140
It allocates by who can afford the best lobbyist. If your disease can't hire a P.R.

51
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:32,380
firm, it's not a real disease. It's just dying. Your Congressman: A Fundraiser Who Occasionally Legislates.

52
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:38,440
Fundraising: four hours a day. Reading bills: zero hours a day.

53
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:43,720
Actual governing: Whatever's left. They don't read what they vote on.

54
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:50,160
The PATRIOT Act: 342 pages, voted on 45 minutes after release.

55
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:56,310
That's 7.6 pages per minute. Olympic speed-readers can't do that.

56
00:05:56,380 --> 00:06:02,020
The Affordable Care Act: 2,700 pages, "we have to pass it to see what's in it".

57
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:09,580
These people control nuclear weapons. Sleep tight. Congressional Committees: The Menu.

58
00:06:10,440 --> 00:06:18,080
Committees control which bills live or die. Both parties assign fundraising quotas for each seat: raise this much for the

59
00:06:18,080 --> 00:06:25,740
party, or lose your assignment. Don't hit your number? Your bills die, your amendments vanish, and your name goes on a literal

60
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:31,000
"wall of shame". It's like a restaurant where the waiters have to pay for their own sections.

61
00:06:32,300 --> 00:06:38,220
Here's the menu. Want to be House Speaker? Raise twenty to thirty-one million dollars for your party.

62
00:06:38,820 --> 00:06:42,560
Want to chair a powerful committee like Ways and Means or Appropriations?

63
00:06:43,220 --> 00:06:48,540
That'll cost you about one point two to one point eight million dollars in party fundraising.

64
00:06:49,380 --> 00:06:56,490
Even a basic freshman member owes a six-figure fundraising quota just to keep their seat at the table.

65
00:06:57,680 --> 00:07:04,740
The House Speaker position costs thirty-one million dollars for Democrats in the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee,

66
00:07:05,080 --> 00:07:13,460
or D.C.C.C., and twenty million dollars for Republicans in the National Republican Congressional Committee, or N.R.C.C.,

67
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:21,360
because it controls the entire agenda. The Ways and Means Chair costs one point eight million dollars for Democrats and

68
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:26,300
one point two million dollars for Republicans because all tax laws start here.

69
00:07:27,060 --> 00:07:33,200
The Appropriations Chair costs one point eight million dollars for Democrats and one point two million dollars for Republicans

70
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:36,920
because it controls the one point seven trillion dollar budget.

71
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:44,040
The Energy and Commerce chair costs one point eight million dollars for Democrats and one point two million dollars for

72
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:51,680
Republicans because it handles healthcare and Wall Street. A Regular Committee seat costs between five hundred thousand

73
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:58,620
and one million dollars for Democrats and eight hundred seventy-five thousand dollars for Republicans, which still beats

74
00:07:58,620 --> 00:08:05,660
irrelevance. A Basic Member owes at least one hundred fifty thousand dollars as an entry fee to play.

75
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:10,560
This is why congressmen spend four hours a day fundraising.

76
00:08:11,180 --> 00:08:18,420
They're not campaigning. They're paying rent. The people writing your healthcare laws literally bought their positions from

77
00:08:18,420 --> 00:08:24,880
the industries those laws regulate. On your planet, this is called "the system working."

78
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:35,539
Public Choice Theory: The Nobel Prize for Cynicism. In 1986, James Buchanan won the Nobel Prize in Economics for formally

79
00:08:35,760 --> 00:08:44,140
proving what anyone who has ever been to the D.M.V. already knew: People don't become angels when you give them a government

80
00:08:44,140 --> 00:08:53,400
I.D. badge. This is Public Choice Theory: bureaucrats, politicians, and regulators are JUST AS SELFISH as everyone else.

81
00:08:54,020 --> 00:08:58,760
The only difference is that when a corporation is greedy, you can stop buying their product.

82
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:03,900
When a bureaucracy is greedy, they can arrest you for not buying their product.

83
00:09:05,140 --> 00:09:15,100
What They Actually Maximize. The N.I.H. should maximize cures, but actually maximizes grants awarded, resulting in no cures.

84
00:09:15,860 --> 00:09:23,680
The F.D.A. should maximize lives saved, but actually maximizes avoiding lawsuits, resulting in deadly delays.

85
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:32,060
Congress should maximize voter welfare, but actually maximizes campaign donations, resulting in corporate welfare.

86
00:09:33,440 --> 00:09:41,360
Pharma should maximize health outcomes, but actually maximizes recurring revenue, meaning chronic disease is preferred.

87
00:09:42,300 --> 00:09:50,080
Defense contractors should maximize national security, but actually maximize government contracts, resulting in endless

88
00:09:50,080 --> 00:09:55,380
wars. NOBODY in the system is incentivized to cure disease.

89
00:09:55,840 --> 00:10:03,860
Or end war. You could elect five hundred thirty-five clones of Gandhi and within two years they'd be taking pharma money.

90
00:10:04,680 --> 00:10:10,980
The system digests idealists like a stomach digests food. It doesn't matter what you put in.

91
00:10:11,440 --> 00:10:20,960
You get the same output. The Libertarian Paradox. Libertarians say: Government fails because government is government.

92
00:10:21,860 --> 00:10:25,840
Socialists say: Government fails because it needs more government.

93
00:10:26,940 --> 00:10:34,120
Public Choice Theory says: Government fails because the people running it are people, and people are terrible.

94
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:43,300
The solution: Stop expecting people to be good. Design systems where being selfish accidentally produces good outcomes.

95
00:10:44,060 --> 00:10:51,500
Like capitalism, but for not dying. Regulatory Capture: How Industries Write Their Own Rules.

96
00:10:52,540 --> 00:10:56,700
There are thirteen thousand seven hundred registered lobbyists in Washington.

97
00:10:57,460 --> 00:11:04,320
Half of them used to work in the government they're now bribing, which on your planet is called "a career path" rather than

98
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:10,980
"corruption." In 2023, corporations spent four point one billion dollars on lobbying.

99
00:11:11,780 --> 00:11:19,560
Divide that by five hundred thirty-five members of Congress and you get seven point seven million dollars in professional

100
00:11:19,560 --> 00:11:25,200
persuasion surrounding each congressman who earns less than a mid-level dentist.

101
00:11:26,160 --> 00:11:30,640
The mystery is not that they're corrupt. The mystery is that any of them aren't.

102
00:11:31,940 --> 00:11:37,600
Citizens United made corporations people who can donate unlimited money through Super PACs.

103
00:11:38,300 --> 00:11:45,500
Corporations are people now, which is odd because you can't imprison a corporation, give it chemotherapy, or make it feel

104
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:48,940
shame. Pharmaceutical companies write their own regulations.

105
00:11:49,660 --> 00:11:54,240
The Medicare Modernization Act banned the government from negotiating drug prices.

106
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:59,100
That's why insulin that costs six dollars to make costs you three hundred dollars.

107
00:11:59,900 --> 00:12:07,090
A five thousand percent markup. Your street-corner drug dealers find this excessive, and they don't even have shareholders.

108
00:12:08,580 --> 00:12:16,700
The military-industrial complex distributes operations across 42 states so that cutting any program threatens local jobs

109
00:12:16,700 --> 00:12:22,910
in enough congressional districts to make it impossible. It's a tumor that grew its own blood supply.

110
00:12:23,640 --> 00:12:29,740
The Pentagon has never passed an audit but somehow knows it needs more money (they can't tell you where the last trillion

111
00:12:29,900 --> 00:12:32,900
went, but they're very confident about needing the next one).

112
00:12:33,780 --> 00:12:38,940
Pharma employs 3 lobbyists for every member of Congress. The F.D.A.

113
00:12:38,940 --> 00:12:43,100
gets seventy-five percent of its drug review budget from the companies it regulates.

114
00:12:43,860 --> 00:12:53,340
For details, see F.D.A.: Unsafe and Ineffective. The system maintains itself with the elegant efficiency of a parasite that

115
00:12:53,340 --> 00:13:00,620
keeps its host alive just enough to keep feeding. Cures are a one-time payment; treatments are recurring revenue.

116
00:13:01,320 --> 00:13:08,640
Approving a bad drug ends careers; delaying a good one ends lives (but not careers, and careers are what matter to people

117
00:13:08,640 --> 00:13:13,520
with careers). Everyone who matters wins. Everyone who's dying loses.

118
00:13:14,220 --> 00:13:18,280
The system works perfectly. It's just optimized for the wrong thing.

119
00:13:19,360 --> 00:13:27,360
The Solution They Don't Want. The whole system runs on one trick: each American loses one hundred dollars to corruption

120
00:13:27,860 --> 00:13:35,220
(not worth a phone call). Each corporation gains one hundred million dollars (worth a thousand phone calls, a lobbyist army,

121
00:13:35,240 --> 00:13:41,160
and a senator's vacation home). But what if curing disease was more profitable than treating it?

122
00:13:41,820 --> 00:13:46,820
What if peace paid better than war? That's what a one percent treaty does.

123
00:13:47,180 --> 00:13:50,720
It doesn't fight the corruption machine. It feeds it different inputs.

124
00:13:51,380 --> 00:13:59,340
You're going to out-bribe the bribers with math. *P.S. - If this chapter depressed you, good.

125
00:13:59,340 --> 00:14:01,900
Depression is the correct response to your political system.

126
00:14:02,300 --> 00:14:08,780
But remember: the same corruption that lets corporations buy politicians also lets you buy them back.

127
00:14:09,580 --> 00:14:16,050
Politicians don't have principles. They have prices. And prices, unlike principles, can be negotiated.*
